Issue: the loop has no per-row TRY/CATCH and no transaction. If a single message has no :30T: tag, or the tag is the last field, SUBSTRING receives a negative length and the whole procedure fails with error 536 in the CATCH, which drops #salida and rethrows. Rows processed earlier in the same call have already been updated to PROC (CMT-FR-1) and are now lost; the bad row itself was not yet marked, so it fails again on the next tick. Because the cursor is ordered by the string alias NF_FECHA_INICIO descending, rows that sort before the bad message are consumed and discarded on every tick, and rows after it are never reached while the message stays in the table. On the C# side the SqlException becomes an empty list (CMT-FR-12), so the only visible symptom is an INFO line saying nothing is pending. Related: @Mensaje VARCHAR(4000) silently truncates a longer NF_MQ_MSG (column type *verify*), and there is no NF_MQ_MSG IS NOT NULL filter, so a NULL message is returned and published as an empty text message (*verify* NMS behaviour for CreateTextMessage(null)).
Fix: parse defensively and isolate each row.
Code block (sql)
SET @Existe1 = CHARINDEX(':30T:', @Mensaje);
SET @queFecha = CASE WHEN @Existe1 > 0 THEN SUBSTRING(@Mensaje, @Existe1 + 5, 8) END;
IF TRY_CONVERT(date, @queFecha, 112) IS NULL
BEGIN  -- quarantine: mark the row ERR with a reason, write the audit row, continue with the next message
    ...
END
Process rows set-based or inside a per-row BEGIN TRY ... END TRY so one bad message cannot affect the others; size @Mensaje to the source column (or VARCHAR(MAX)), filter NULL, and make the quarantine visible to operations (alert). Benefit: a bad message is isolated and reported instead of silently consuming every message ahead of it. Suggested JIRA: CMT-2 (Major)
