5. Architecture-Fit Note
Applying the standing question in section 6 of the control document (./_ORCHESTRATION.md): this service is a timer-driven polling publisher over a table used as an outbox (TB_SRV_SWF_TRABAJO rows of type MT-300, NF_MQ_MSG_ETL IS NULL -> EXITOSO) that hands each row to an ActiveMQ queue; the work per message is one publish plus one status update. That is already the "message-driven" shape of the trade-off table, and the only reason it is fragile is that the outbox contract is implemented backwards (the read consumes the row, CMT-FR-1) and neither side is atomic. There is nothing multi-step with compensations here, so a workflow/saga engine, serverless function or microservice split would add cost without addressing the defects. The recommended sequence is the portfolio one: (1) make the read non-destructive and the mark verifiable (CMT-FR-1, 2, 3), surface read/send/mark failures (CMT-FR-5, 6, 12, 13), keep payloads out of logs (CMT-FR-14) and make the build reproducible (CMT-FR-19); (2) make the test project compile and cover the failure paths (CMT-FR-22, 23) and extract the shared scaffolding it duplicates (CMT-FR-10); (3) only then decide on hosting: modernise in place (.NET 8 worker, same layering) or replace the table poll with a transactional outbox/CDC publisher. That decision needs facts this audit cannot see: the volume and peak rate of MT300, the real timer interval (held in the encrypted configuration), the latency Calypso expects, whether the Calypso consumer de-duplicates by folio, broker URI options and failover policy, and the hosting constraints. A full rewrite is not recommended from a code-quality review alone.
